'' =============================================================================
''  test-F256  --  is `_RET_ CALL #helper` equivalent to `CALL #helper` + `RET`?
'' =============================================================================
''  QUESTION (F-256).  The XBYTE Programming Guide says at xbyte-body.md:879
''  that "Chapter 15's _RET_ CALL #set_nz idiom depends entirely on" skip
''  suspension during a call, and uses the idiom at :416, :793, :1391, :1400.
''  `_ret_ call #x` ASSEMBLES CLEAN, so the community objection "you cannot
''  combine a CALL with ret" is wrong as stated -- but the compiler proves
''  LEGALITY, NOT SEMANTICS.  When one instruction both pushes a return
''  address and returns, does control reach the helper AND resume XBYTE
''  dispatch at $1FF?
''
''  ROUND 3 -- WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY
''
''  1. THE TEST IS NOW DIFFERENTIAL, NOT ABSOLUTE.
''     Earlier rounds asked "does a counter keep climbing", which tests against
''     an EXPECTATION.  This round runs the same work two ways:
''         $01  h_retcall   ... _ret_ call #helper   (return ON the call line)
''         $04  h_callret   ... call #helper / ret   (return AFTER it)
''     These are supposed to be the same thing.  If their trails match, the
''     idiom is proven EQUIVALENT by construction.  If they diverge, the
''     divergence itself localises the defect.  $04 is the reference; nothing
''     is concluded from $01 alone.
''
''  2. A TRAIL, NOT A COUNTER.  A count says how far it got; it cannot say
''     whether the callee was ever entered.  Every handler and the helper
''     APPEND a token, so we read execution ORDER:
''         caller stamp   -- the handler was entered
''         callee stamp   -- the helper actually ran, tagged with WHO called it
''         continuation   -- the next bytecode's handler appended its own token
''     Which separates the failure modes:
''         no caller stamp .................. dispatch broke BEFORE the idiom;
''                                            the idiom is untested, not
''                                            disproven
''         caller, no callee ................ the CALL half never happened
''         caller + callee, trail ends ...... the RET half did not re-enter
''                                            XBYTE at $1FF
''         caller + callee + continuation ... it works -- and if the trail
''                                            matches $04's, it is equivalent
''
''  3. THE RETURN ADDRESS IS MEASURED, NOT INFERRED.  The helper pops the
''     hardware stack, records what the CALL pushed, and pushes it straight
''     back.  So "returned to $1FF" becomes a number we read instead of
''     something we deduce from dispatch continuing.
''
''  4. ADDRESS RESOLUTION IS A GATE, NOT A VARIABLE.  See below.
''
''  THE ADDRESS BUG THAT VOIDED ROUNDS 1-2 (proven statically, no bench)
''  `orgh` inside a Spin2 DAT assigns hub addresses from $400 -- the STANDALONE
''  PASM convention, where the image really is loaded at hub $0.  In a Spin2
''  program that is false: the object lands high (~$1888 in a build like this
''  one).  Decoding the emitted AUGS+MOV pair for a test file proved it: a
''  table whose runtime address is $018A4 encodes as ##value $00414, which is
''  exactly $400 + the size of the preceding org code; adding one instruction
''  moved BOTH by 4.  So `##disp` names low hub memory -- interpreter and
''  debugger territory -- which is why LUT[0] came back $F603_9BD5 and why the
''  "first bytecode" read $5E on one run and $F8 on the next: that region is
''  live, changing memory.
''  This does NOT make `##hubsym` a broken idiom.  In a pure-PASM top-level
''  file its precondition holds.  Moving the DAT into a Spin2 object silently
''  removes it.
''  So: every VM arm takes its table and program addresses from Spin2 via `@`,
''  held constant, and PASS 1 measures `@disp` against `##disp` ONCE, off to
''  the side, consuming nothing.  If those two agree the whole story is dead
''  and I start over.
''
''  Note the dispatch TABLE ENTRIES stay bare cog addresses -- a LUT entry is
''  [9:0] routine address + [31:10] SKIPF pattern, and EXECF cannot use a hub
''  address.  It is the table's OWN hub address that needs `@`.
''
''  Build: pnut-ts -d test-f256-retcall-xbyte.spin2
''  Rig:   bare board.  No jumpers.  (VO-J class.)
'' =============================================================================

CON
  _clkfreq = 200_000_000

  '' CONFIRMING RUN. The round-3 result -- that _RET_ CALL falls through
  '' instead of returning to XBYTE -- was measured BEFORE we found that
  '' DEBUG_COGS defaults to %11111111, putting the P2's highest-priority
  '' interrupt inside every launched cog. That confound wrecked our streamer
  '' measurements (F-266). XBYTE is another hardware sequencer and I have no
  '' basis for assuming it is immune, so this re-run restricts debug to cog 0.
  '' The sprint plan's own §3 error clause requires confirming a "the idiom is
  '' broken" result by an independent path before restructuring XBYTE §15.3.
  DEBUG_COGS = %0000_0001

  ' trail tokens
  T_PLAIN   = $A0
  T_RETCALL = $A1                        ' return ON the call line
  T_BREAK   = $A2
  T_HALT    = $A3
  T_CALLRET = $A4                        ' return AFTER the call line
  T_HELP_RC = $B1                        ' helper ran, invoked by _RET_ CALL
  T_HELP_CR = $B4                        ' helper ran, invoked by CALL + RET

  ' normalised forms, so the two arms can be compared element by element
  N_CALLER  = $AA
  N_HELPER  = $BB

DAT
s_plain         byte    "h_plain", 0
s_retcall       byte    "h_retcall  (_RET_ CALL)", 0
s_callret       byte    "h_callret  (CALL + RET)", 0
s_break         byte    "h_break    (leaves XBYTE)", 0
s_halt          byte    "h_halt", 0
s_help_rc       byte    "  -> helper, called by _RET_ CALL", 0
s_help_cr       byte    "  -> helper, called by CALL + RET", 0
s_unknown       byte    "???", 0

VAR
  long blk[32]
  '  [0]      trail count
  '  [1..15]  trail tokens
  '  [16]     @disp   (in)
  '  [17]     @prog   (in)
  '  [18]     address Spin2 handed the cog
  '  [19]     value of PASM's ##disp
  '  [20][21] LUT[0], LUT[1] loaded via the Spin2 address
  '  [22]     first bytecode read from the FIFO
  '  [23]     return address the helper saw (_RET_ CALL arm)
  '  [24]     return address the helper saw (CALL + RET arm)
  '  [25]     done
  '  [26]     plain-cog harness counter
  '  [27][28] LUT[0], LUT[1] loaded via ##disp
  long trailA[16], cntA, radr_rc
  long trailB[16], cntB, radr_cr


PUB main()

  debug("=== F-256 round 3: _RET_ CALL vs CALL+RET, by trail ===")

  '' ---- PASS -1: can a plain cog run and write hub at all? ----------------
  longfill(@blk[0], 0, 32)
  coginit(NEWCOG, @engine_null, @blk[0])
  waitms(50)
  debug("PASS -1  plain cog, no XBYTE: count=", udec_(blk[26]), " (expect 3)")
  if blk[26] <> 3
    debug("*** THE HARNESS ITSELF IS BROKEN. Nothing about XBYTE can be said.")
    stop_here()
  debug("         harness sound: coginit, PTRA and hub writes all land.")
  debug(" ")

  '' ---- PASS 1: the address gate -----------------------------------------
  longfill(@blk[0], 0, 32)
  blk[16] := @disp
  coginit(NEWCOG, @engine_addr, @blk[0])
  waitms(50)
  debug("PASS 1   ADDRESS CHECK")
  debug("         Spin2 @disp = ", uhex_long_(blk[18]), "     PASM ##disp = ", uhex_long_(blk[19]))
  debug("         via @disp : LUT[0]=", uhex_long_(blk[20]), " LUT[1]=", uhex_long_(blk[21]))
  debug("         via ##disp: LUT[0]=", uhex_long_(blk[27]), " LUT[1]=", uhex_long_(blk[28]))
  debug("         (expect small cog addresses; handlers live below \$100)")
  if blk[18] == blk[19]
    debug("         ADDRESSES AGREE -- the orgh/\$400 story is DEAD. Start over.")
  else
    debug("         they differ by ", udec_(blk[18] - blk[19]), " bytes, as predicted:")
    debug("         orgh in a Spin2 DAT numbers from \$400, not from the object.")
  debug(" ")

  '' ---- PASS 2: the reference arm, CALL + RET ----------------------------
  '' Run the REFERENCE first. If the known-good long form cannot dispatch,
  '' nothing can be concluded about the idiom, and we say so instead of
  '' reporting numbers.
  run_vm(@progCR)
  cntB := blk[0]
  longmove(@trailB[0], @blk[1], 15)
  debug("PASS 2   REFERENCE  \$00 \$04 \$00 \$03   (call #helper / ret)")
  report()
  radr_cr := blk[24]
  debug("         helper saw return address ", uhex_long_(radr_cr))

  if cntB < 3
    debug("*** THE REFERENCE ARM DID NOT DISPATCH.")
    debug("*** The rig cannot run a VM at all, so _RET_ CALL is UNTESTED --")
    debug("*** not disproven. Fix the rig before reading anything below.")
    stop_here()

  '' ---- PASS 3: the arm under test, _RET_ CALL ---------------------------
  run_vm(@progRC)
  cntA := blk[0]
  longmove(@trailA[0], @blk[1], 15)
  debug(" ")
  debug("PASS 3   UNDER TEST  \$00 \$01 \$00 \$03   (_ret_ call #helper)")
  report()
  radr_rc := blk[23]
  debug("         helper saw return address ", uhex_long_(radr_rc))

  '' ---- PASS 4: the control that MUST fail -------------------------------
  run_vm(@progBK)
  debug(" ")
  debug("PASS 4   CONTROL  \$00 \$02 \$00 \$03   (\$02 jumps out of XBYTE)")
  report()
  if blk[0] <> 2
    debug("*** CONTROL DID NOT FAIL. A handler that leaves XBYTE still let")
    debug("*** dispatch continue, so the trail is lying and PASS 2 and PASS 3")
    debug("*** are both VOID.")
    stop_here()
  debug("         control failed exactly as it must: trail stops at the break.")

  verdict()
  stop_here()


PRI verdict() | i, same
  debug(" ")
  debug("--- VERDICT ---")

  same := true
  if cntA <> cntB
    debug("TRAIL LENGTHS DIFFER: _RET_ CALL ", udec_(cntA), " vs CALL+RET ", udec_(cntB))
    same := false
  else
    repeat i from 0 to cntA - 1
      if norm(trailA[i]) <> norm(trailB[i])
        debug("TRAILS DIVERGE at step ", udec_(i + 1), ": ", uhex_byte_(trailA[i]), " vs ", uhex_byte_(trailB[i]))
        same := false

  if same
    debug("THE TWO FORMS PRODUCE IDENTICAL TRAILS.")
    debug("`_RET_ CALL #helper` reaches the helper AND resumes XBYTE dispatch,")
    debug("exactly as `CALL #helper` followed by `RET` does. The idiom at")
    debug("xbyte-body.md :416 :793 :1391 :1400 is sound, and the community")
    debug("objection is wrong in substance as well as in form.")
    if radr_rc == radr_cr
      debug("Both forms pushed the same return address, too.")
    else
      debug("NOTE: the pushed return addresses differ -- same outcome,")
      debug("      different mechanism. Worth documenting in the guide.")
  else
    debug("THE TWO FORMS ARE NOT EQUIVALENT. Read the trails above: the step")
    debug("where they part is the defect, and the guide's idiom is implicated.")


PRI norm(tk) : n
  n := tk
  if tk == T_RETCALL or tk == T_CALLRET
    n := N_CALLER
  if tk == T_HELP_RC or tk == T_HELP_CR
    n := N_HELPER


PRI report() | i
  debug("         first bytecode from FIFO = ", uhex_byte_(blk[22]), " (expect \$00)")
  debug("         trail (", udec_(blk[0]), " steps):")
  repeat i from 1 to blk[0]
    debug("           ", udec_(i), ": ", uhex_byte_(blk[i]), "  ", zstr_(name_of(blk[i])))


PRI name_of(tk) : p
  case tk
    T_PLAIN:   p := @s_plain
    T_RETCALL: p := @s_retcall
    T_CALLRET: p := @s_callret
    T_BREAK:   p := @s_break
    T_HALT:    p := @s_halt
    T_HELP_RC: p := @s_help_rc
    T_HELP_CR: p := @s_help_cr
    other:     p := @s_unknown


PRI run_vm(prog_addr) | cogn, tstart
  longfill(@blk[0], 0, 26)
  blk[16] := @disp
  blk[17] := prog_addr
  cogn := coginit(NEWCOG, @engine_vm, @blk[0])
  tstart := getct()
  repeat until blk[25] == 1 or (getct() - tstart) > (clkfreq / 4)
  if blk[25] <> 1
    cogstop(cogn)
    debug("         *** VM did not finish within 250 ms (dispatch hung)")


PRI stop_here()
  debug(" ")
  debug("@@@ TEST COMPLETE: F-256 retcall-xbyte @@@")
  debug("@@@ nothing further will print - safe to stop the session @@@")
  repeat


'' ===========================================================================
DAT             org
'' engine_null -- no XBYTE.  Proves coginit, PTRA and hub writes work.
engine_null
                mov     pn, ptra
                add     pn, #26*4
                mov     tn, #3
.lp             rdlong  vn, pn
                add     vn, #1
                wrlong  vn, pn
                djnz    tn, #.lp
                cogid   tn
                cogstop tn
pn              res     1
tn              res     1
vn              res     1


'' ===========================================================================
DAT             org
'' engine_addr -- the gate.  Reports both addresses and what each one loads.
engine_addr
                mov     pa_, ptra

                mov     ta_, pa_
                add     ta_, #16*4
                rdlong  ta_, ta_                ' the address Spin2 handed us
                mov     tq_, pa_
                add     tq_, #18*4
                wrlong  ta_, tq_

                mov     tb_, ##disp             ' what PASM thinks it is
                mov     tq_, pa_
                add     tq_, #19*4
                wrlong  tb_, tq_

                setq2   #8-1                    ' load via Spin2's address
                rdlong  $000, ta_
                rdlut   tc_, #0
                mov     tq_, pa_
                add     tq_, #20*4
                wrlong  tc_, tq_
                rdlut   tc_, #1
                add     tq_, #4
                wrlong  tc_, tq_

                mov     tc_, #0                 ' wipe, so the second load
                wrlut   tc_, #0                 ' cannot inherit the first
                wrlut   tc_, #1

                setq2   #8-1                    ' load via PASM's ##disp
                rdlong  $000, tb_
                rdlut   tc_, #0
                mov     tq_, pa_
                add     tq_, #27*4
                wrlong  tc_, tq_
                rdlut   tc_, #1
                add     tq_, #4
                wrlong  tc_, tq_

                cogid   tq_
                cogstop tq_
pa_             res     1
ta_             res     1
tb_             res     1
tc_             res     1
tq_             res     1


'' ===========================================================================
DAT             org
'' engine_vm -- ONE cog image, run three times with different programs, so the
'' bytecode stream is the only thing that varies between arms.
engine_vm
                mov     pblk, ptra
                mov     tv, #0

                mov     tq, pblk                ' dispatch table -> LUT $000
                add     tq, #16*4
                rdlong  tq, tq
                setq2   #8-1
                rdlong  $000, tq

                mov     tq, pblk                ' bytecode stream -> FIFO
                add     tq, #17*4
                rdlong  tprog, tq
                rdfast  #0, tprog
                rfbyte  tc                      ' peek the first bytecode
                mov     tq, pblk
                add     tq, #22*4
                wrlong  tc, tq
                rdfast  #0, tprog               ' re-point after the peek

                push    #$1ff                   ' XBYTE re-entry target
        _ret_   setq    #0                      ' arm: 256 entries @ LUT $000

'' --- handlers --------------------------------------------------------------
h_plain         mov     tok, #T_PLAIN
                call    #append
        _ret_   or      tv, #0                  ' the plain _RET_ form

h_retcall       mov     tok, #T_RETCALL         ' caller stamp
                call    #append
                mov     hid, #T_HELP_RC
                mov     hslot, #23
        _ret_   call    #helper                 ' <=== THE IDIOM UNDER TEST

h_callret       mov     tok, #T_CALLRET         ' caller stamp
                call    #append
                mov     hid, #T_HELP_CR
                mov     hslot, #24
                call    #helper                 ' the same work, long form
                ret

h_break         mov     tok, #T_BREAK
                call    #append
                jmp     #vm_stop                ' deliberately leaves XBYTE

h_halt          mov     tok, #T_HALT
                call    #append
                jmp     #vm_stop

'' --- the callee ------------------------------------------------------------
'' Records what the CALL actually pushed, then puts it straight back so the
'' $1FF underneath is never disturbed.
helper          pop     tret
                push    tret
                mov     tok, hid
                call    #append
                mov     tq, pblk
                mov     tw, hslot
                shl     tw, #2
                add     tq, tw
                wrlong  tret, tq
                ret

'' --- trail append ----------------------------------------------------------
append          mov     tq, pblk
                rdlong  tc, tq                  ' current count
                cmp     tc, #15         wc      ' room left?
        if_nc   ret                             ' full: drop, never corrupt
                add     tc, #1
                wrlong  tc, tq
                mov     tw, tc
                shl     tw, #2
                add     tq, tw
                wrlong  tok, tq
                ret

vm_stop         mov     tq, pblk
                add     tq, #25*4
                wrlong  #1, tq
                cogid   tq
                cogstop tq

pblk            res     1
tq              res     1
tc              res     1
tw              res     1
tok             res     1
tv              res     1
hid             res     1
hslot           res     1
tret            res     1
tprog           res     1


'' ===========================================================================
DAT
'' Dispatch table and the three bytecode streams.  A LUT entry is
'' [9:0] routine address + [31:10] SKIPF pattern; our skip pattern is 0, so a
'' bare cog address is the whole entry.  These are COG addresses on purpose --
'' EXECF cannot use a hub address.
                orgh
disp            long    h_plain                 ' $00
                long    h_retcall               ' $01
                long    h_break                 ' $02
                long    h_halt                  ' $03
                long    h_callret               ' $04
                long    h_plain                 ' $05
                long    h_plain                 ' $06
                long    h_plain                 ' $07

progRC          byte    $00, $01, $00, $03      ' plain, _RET_ CALL, plain, halt
progCR          byte    $00, $04, $00, $03      ' plain, CALL+RET,   plain, halt
progBK          byte    $00, $02, $00, $03      ' plain, break,      plain, halt
